From: Nico Weber <thakis@chromium.org>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 14:45:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Set LIBC_MATH to make LLVM's libm faster on special inputs

V8 never looks at errno or the floating-point exception flags after
calling a math function, and always runs in round-to-nearest mode. So
tell LLVM's libm that it doesn't have to set errno, raise exceptions, or
handle other rounding modes.

This makes inputs with special results much faster: inf, nan, 0,
out-of-domain inputs, and inputs where the result saturates to an
inexact constant (tanh(x) = +/-1 for |x| >= 20, expm1(x) = -1 for x <=
-38). Accuracy is unaffected.

These are likely hit rarely in the real world! But there's no downside
to setting this.

Normal inputs are mostly unaffected, but log, log1p, log2, and log10 get
about 4-7% faster: their error paths called out-of-line helpers to set
errno and raise exceptions, so they weren't leaf functions and set up a
stack frame on every call. Now they are, and log and log2 also get
inlined into base::ieee754.

Measured with weird-cli.js from js-math-bench [1], median of 7 runs.
acosh, asinh, atanh, cbrt, hypot, pow, and sqrt don't change.

